WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.440
How does an xv6 user

2
00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.620
command reach the kernel? Follow

3
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.780
two connected routes: building the

4
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.600
command into the file-system image,

5
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.880
then crossing from user execution

6
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.680
to a registered kernel service.

7
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.120
This companion explains the manual

8
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.380
mechanisms without claiming unverified branch-specific

9
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.180
line numbers.

10
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.560
A user source file alone

11
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:22.860
does not install a command

12
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.240
in xv6. The program must

13
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.920
compile, link with the user

14
00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.840
library, and enter the image

15
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.600
through the Makefile UPROGS list.

16
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.500
If the binary is absent

17
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.120
from the image, the shell

18
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:35.800
cannot execute it just because

19
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.100
its source exists on the

20
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:37.400
host.

21
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.860
The user declaration lets compiled

22
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.100
code call the interface, while

23
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.880
the generated assembly stub performs

24
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.540
the machine-level transition. The system-call

25
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.860
number identifies the requested service.

26
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.640
Keep the user function name

27
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.700
and the dispatcher number consistent

28
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.660
across registration.

29
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.080
In the RISC-V route used

30
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.680
by this lab, the stub

31
00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.600
places the system-call number in

32
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.420
register a seven and executes

33
00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.760
ecall. Trap handling enters the

34
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.820
protected kernel path and selects

35
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.040
the registered handler. The returned

36
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.360
value is delivered through a

37
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.620
zero in the saved user

38
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:11.480
register state.

39
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.560
Tracing the existing process-identifier call

40
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.560
provides a registration example before

41
00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.940
adding the process-count service. The

42
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.260
new service needs its user

43
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.600
declaration, generated stub, number, kernel

44
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.700
dispatch entry and handler connected.

45
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.740
A missing link can cause

46
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.740
a build error, an unrecognized

47
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.720
request, or an incorrect result.

48
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:34.380
The manual defines getproccount as

49
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.960
counting table entries whose state

50
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.480
is not UNUSED at the

51
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.120
instant each entry is inspected.

52
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.140
Zombies are therefore counted while

53
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:42.820
their table entries remain allocated.

54
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.140
That definition differs from counting

55
00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.240
only processes currently executing on

56
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:47.820
a processor.

57
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.300
A table scan with per-entry

58
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.180
inspection is not a globally

59
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:55.400
atomic process snapshot. Processes may

60
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.880
change state while the scan

61
00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.160
proceeds, depending on its synchronization.

62
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:00.980
The count can differ from

63
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.780
another listing sampled through a

64
00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.760
different observation path or time.

65
00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.620
Things to take note of.

66
00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.620
UPROGS concerns installation, ecall concerns

67
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.880
the privilege crossing, and registration

68
00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.380
connects the number to the

69
00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.800
service. Explain the precise count

70
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.400
semantics and use your actual

71
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.200
lab branch before citing exact

72
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.080
source locations.
